cts is not established."]}],"facts":[{"id":"F_prepare_metadata","meaning":"On the noncommitted reply branch, the constructed PrepareReply reports the instance's current bal, vbal, commands, local default ballot, and acceptor identity after this handler's conditional update. It does not by itself establish successful preparation at the request ballot.","identity":{"replica":"r.Id / PrepareReply.AcceptorId","instance":"PrepareReply.Instance","reported_context":"Ballot, VBallot, DefaultBallot, and Command in this reply"},"established_by":["B_prepare"],"consumed_by":["B_prepare_reply"],"validity_context":"Metadata snapshot when the reply object is constructed; delivery and continued local-state validity are separate.","representation":["PrepareReply fields"],"invalidators":[],"reinterpreters":["B_prepare_reply"],"durability":"No persistence is performed by handlePrepare.","recovery":"Subsequent prepare handling can report current metadata again; restart reconstruction is unknown.","source_ids":["paxos/paxos.go:398:538","paxos/paxos.go:539:666"],"unknowns":["Message serialization and provenance","Whether request and reply paths preserve the intended ballot context","Transport filtering of repeated replies"]},{"id":"F_accept_reply_metadata","meaning":"The constructed AcceptReply reports the addressed instance's current bal after handleAccept. The handler produces this report whether it installed the request, rejected a lower ballot, or retained a committed record.","identity":{"instance":"AcceptReply.Instance","reported_ballot":"AcceptReply.Ballot","reporting_replica":"Handling replica; identity association outside these visible reply fields is unknown"},"established_by":["B_accept"],"consumed_by":["B_accept_reply"],"validity_context":"Observation at reply construction; it carries no visible command identity or explicit accepted/rejected flag.","representation":["AcceptReply{accept.Instance, inst.bal}"],"invalidators":[],"reinterpreters":["B_accept_reply"],"durability":"Installation branches invoke persistence helpers, but this report does not establish durable completion.","recovery":"No reply recovery mechanism is visible.","source_ids":["paxos/paxos.go:398:538","paxos/paxos.go:539:666"],"unknowns":["Sender identity available to dispatch","Unique-participant accounting","Which producer branches can occur for an equal-ballot reply in a legal execution"]},{"id":"F_counted_commit_marker","meaning":"The local instance was marked COMMITTED by handleAcceptReply after its equal-ballot reply counter plus one reached WriteQuorumSize. This fact records the implemented counter transition and does not assert distinct-participant support.","identity":{"replica_instance":"r.Id and areply.Instance","attempt":"lb.lastTriedBallot at threshold","local_record":"Instance commands and ballot retained when status changes"},"established_by":["B_accept_reply"],"consumed_by":["B_execute_batch"],"validity_context":"Local record immediately following the threshold transition; command establishment and reply uniqueness require separate evidence.","representation":["Instance.status == COMMITTED","LeaderBookkeeping.acceptOKs"],"invalidators":[],"reinterpreters":["B_execute_batch"],"durability":"Metadata recording and sync are invoked, conditionally on Durable, without checked completion.","recovery":"No restart reader is supplied.","source_ids":["paxos/paxos.go:539:666","paxos/paxos.go:667:736"],"unknowns":["Other writers in the unread event and ballot paths","Command/attempt association and self-count establishment","Broadcast recipient selection"]}],"surfaces":[{"entry_point":"Replica.handlePrepare and Replica.handleAccept","disposition":"mapped","behavior_ids":["B_prepare","B_accept"],"reason":"Local mutation and reply construction are mapped; message ingress and sender-side legality remain open.","source_ids":["paxos/paxos.go:398:538"],"high_consequence":true},{"entry_point":"Replica.handlePrepareReply and Replica.handleAcceptReply","disposition":"mapped","behavior_ids":["B_prepare_reply","B_accept_reply"],"reason":"Visible ballot filters, counters, and progression branches are mapped without inferring unique quorum support.","source_ids":["paxos/paxos.go:539:666"],"high_consequence":true},{"entry_point":"Replica.executeCommands and Replica.recover","disposition":"mapped","behavior_ids":["B_execute_batch","B_request_recovery","B_recover_attempt"],"reason":"Application, queued recovery requests, and recovery initiation are distinct behaviors; handoff dispatch remains unknown.","source_ids":["paxos/paxos.go:667:736"],"high_consequence":true},{"entry_point":"Replica.run dispatch and proposal throttling","disposition":"mapped","behavior_ids":["paxos_run_dispatch"],"reason":"The attached loop establishes startup calls, asynchronous owners, synchronous channel dispatch, and batching-channel control. Callee protocol semantics remain separately deferred.","source_ids":["paxos/paxos.go:24:160","paxos/paxos.go:161:340"],"high_consequence":true}]},"directed_question":null,"parameters":{},"materials":[{"id":"paxos/paxos.go:24:160","file":"paxos/paxos.go","start_line":24,"end_line":160,"kind":"code_observation","content_digest":"0386f6cba4a8ae0791dc9acdd3279cb13d9ca657eb1723e286d946cfde9f6edb","source_view_id":"source-view-2"},{"id":"paxos/paxos.go:667:736","file":"paxos/paxos.go","start_line":667,"end_line":736,"kind":"code_observation","content_digest":"0386f6cba4a8ae0791dc9acdd3279cb13d9ca657eb1723e286d946cfde9f6edb","source_view_id":"source-view-2"},{"id":"paxos/paxos.go:161:397","file":"paxos/paxos.go","start_line":161,"end_line":397,"kind":"code_observation","content_digest":"0386f6cba4a8ae0791dc9acdd3279cb13d9ca657eb1723e286d946cfde9f6edb","source_view_id":"source-view-2"},{"id":"paxos/paxos.go:398:666","file":"paxos/paxos.go","start_line":398,"end_line":666,"kind":"code_observation","content_digest":"0386f6cba4a8ae0791dc9acdd3279cb13d9ca657eb1723e286d946cfde9f6edb","source_view_id":"source-view-2"},{"id":"replica/replica.go:1:300","file":"replica/replica.go","start_line":1,"end_line":300,"kind":"code_observation","content_digest":"ea28e2b94198a705e6dd9892a6154ce9b8cb424ec3dedeed8f8bd2488341e766","source_view_id":"source-view-3"},{"id":"replica/replica.go:301:577","file":"replica/replica.go","start_line":301,"end_line":577,"kind":"code_observation","content_digest":"ea28e2b94198a705e6dd9892a6154ce9b8cb424ec3dedeed8f8bd2488341e766","source_view_id":"source-view-3"},{"id":"run.go:1:103","file":"run.go","start_line":1,"end_line":103,"kind":"code_observation","text":"package main\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"log\"\n\t\"net\"\n\t\"net/http\"\n\t\"net/rpc\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\t\"github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos/config\"\n\t\"github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos/curp\"\n\t\"github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos/dlog\"\n\t\"github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos/epaxos\"\n\t\"github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos/fastpaxos\"\n\t\"github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos/n2paxos\"\n\t\"github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos/paxos\"\n\t\"github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos/replica/defs\"\n\t\"github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos/swift\"\n)\n\nfunc runReplica(c *config.Config, logger *dlog.Logger) {\n\tport := c.Port\n\n\tlog.Printf(\"Server starting on port %d\", port)\n\tmaddr := fmt.Sprintf(\"%s:%d\", c.MasterAddr, c.MasterPort)\n\taddr := c.ReplicaAddrs[c.Alias]\n\treplicaId, nodeList, isLeader := registerWithMaster(addr, maddr, port)\n\tf := (len(c.ReplicaAddrs) - 1) / 2\n\tlog.Printf(\"Tolerating %d max. failures\", f)\n\n\tswitch strings.ToLower(c.Protocol) {\n\tcase \"swiftpaxos\":\n\t\tlog.Println(\"Starting SwiftPaxos replica...\")\n\t\tswift.MaxDescRoutines = 100\n\t\trep := swift.New(c.Alias, replicaId, nodeList, !c.Noop,\n\t\t\tc.Optread, true, false, 1, f, c, logger, nil)\n\t\trpc.Register(rep)\n\tcase \"curp\":\n\t\tlog.Println(\"Starting optimized CURP replica...\")\n\t\tcurp.MaxDescRoutines = 100\n\t\trep := curp.New(c.Alias, replicaId, nodeList, !c.Noop,\n\t\t\t1, f, true, c, logger)\n\t\trpc.Register(rep)\n\tcase \"fastpaxos\":\n\t\tlog.Println(\"Starting Fast Paxos replica...\")\n\t\trep := fastpaxos.New(c.Alias, replicaId, nodeList, !c.Noop, f, c, logger)\n\t\trpc.Register(rep)\n\tcase \"n2paxos\":\n\t\tlog.Println(\"Starting N²Paxos replica...\")\n\t\trep := n2paxos.New(c.Alias, replicaId, nodeList, !c.Noop, 1, f, c, logger)\n\t\trpc.Register(rep)\n\tcase \"paxos\":\n\t\tlog.Println(\"Starting Paxos replica...\")\n\t\trep := paxos.New(c.Alias, replicaId, nodeList, isLeader, f, c, logger)\n\t\trpc.Register(rep)\n\tcase \"epaxos\":\n\t\tlog.Println(\"Starting EPaxos replica...\")\n\t\trep := epaxos.New(c.Alias, replicaId, nodeList, !c.Noop, false, false, 0, false, f, c, logger)\n\t\trpc.Register(rep)\n\t}\n\n\trpc.HandleHTTP()\n\tl, err := net.Listen(\"tcp\", fmt.Sprintf(\":%d\", port+1000))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tlog.Fatal(\"listen error:\", err)\n\t}\n\thttp.Serve(l, nil)\n}\n\nfunc registerWithMaster(addr, mAddr string, port int) (int, []string, bool) {\n\tvar reply defs.RegisterReply\n\targs := &defs.RegisterArgs{\n\t\tAddr: addr,\n\t\tPort: port,\n\t}\n\tlog.Printf(\"connecting to: %v\", mAddr)\n\n\tfor {\n\t\tmcli, err := rpc.DialHTTP(\"tcp\", mAddr)\n\t\tif err == nil {\n\t\t\tfor {\n\t\t\t\t// TODO: This is an active wait...\n\t\t\t\terr = mcli.Call(\"Master.Register\", args, &reply)\n\t\t\t\tif err == nil {\n\t\t\t\t\tif reply.Ready {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t\ttime.Sleep(4)\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\tlog.Printf(\"%v\", err)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tlog.Printf(\"%v\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\ttime.Sleep(4)\n\t}\n\n\treturn reply.ReplicaId, reply.NodeList, reply.IsLeader\n}","content_digest":"f54ea73326e8d371b004d6a534e5b61606a190f40e475e2b6e2387a3199e43f8"},{"id":"README.md:1:86","file":"README.md","start_line":1,"end_line":86,"kind":"document_statement","text":"SwiftPaxos: Fast Geo-Replicated State Machines\n==========\n<!-- [![Go Report Card](https://goreportcard.com/badge/github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos)](https://goreportcard.com/report/github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos) -->\n\nThis repository contains the prototype implementation of SwiftPaxos, a new state-machine replication protocol for geo-distributed systems.\nSwiftPaxos is a _faster Paxos without compromises_.\nIn the best case, it executes a state-machine command in two message delays (one round-trip), and three otherwise.\nSwiftPaxos was [presented](https://www.usenix.org/conference/nsdi24/presentation/ryabinin) at the 21st USENIX Symposium on Networked Systems Design and Implementation ([NSDI '24](https://www.usenix.org/conference/nsdi24)).\n\nInstallation\n------------\n\n    git clone https://github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos.git\n    cd swiftpaxos\n    go install github.com/imdea-software/swiftpaxos\n\nImplemented protocols\n---------------------\n\n|  Protocol               | Comments                                          |\n|-------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| SwiftPaxos              | See our NSDI'24 [paper](https://www.usenix.org/conference/nsdi24/presentation/ryabinin) for the full details.|\n| Paxos                   | The classic Paxos protocol.                       |\n| N<sup>2</sup>Paxos      | All-to-all variant of Paxos.                      |\n| CURP                    | CURP implemented over N<sup>2</sup>Paxos.         |\n| Fast Paxos              | Fast Paxos with uncoordinated collision recovery. |\n| EPaxos                  | A [corrected][epaxos_correct] version of EPaxos.  |\n\nThis software is based on the [Egalitarian Paxos](https://github.com/otrack/epaxos) code base, as well as the corrections made [here](https://github.com/otrack/epaxos).\n\nUsage\n-----\n#### participants\nThere are three types of participants: *master*, *servers* and *clients*. \nThe servers and clients implement the protocol logic. \nThe master maintains the configuration of the system.\n\n#### deployment configuration\nTo setup a run, the participants read deployment configuration file. \nSee [aws.conf][config] for an example of configuration file for AWS EC2.\n\n#### launching a participant\n\nMaster:\n    \n    swiftpaxos -run master -config conf.conf\n\nServer:\n\n    swiftpaxos -run server -config conf.conf -alias server_name\n\nClient:\n\n    swiftpaxos -run client -config conf.conf -alias client_name\n\n#### command line options\n\n    -alias alias\n        An alias of this participant\n    -config file\n        Deployment config file (required)\n    -latency file\n        Latency config file\n    -log file\n        Path to the log file\n    -protocol protocol\n        Protocol to run. Overwrites protocol field of the config file\n    -quorum file\n        Quorum config file\n    -run participant\n        Run a participant\n\nSee [quorum.conf][quorum] and [latency.conf][latency] for an example of quorum and latency configuration files.\n\nFlint\n-----\n\nTo have an idea on how different replication protocols would compare, we wrote a tool named [Flint][flint]. \nFlint takes as input a set of AWS regions.\nIt computes the expected latencies and estimates how the protocols perform in such a deployment.\n\n[config]: aws.conf\n[epaxos_correct]: https://github.com/otrack/on-epaxos-correctness\n[quorum]: quorum.conf\n[latency]: latency.conf\n[flint]: https://github.com/vonaka/flint","content_digest":"75ad43f329f505f9c674ae19c35b72c236962ad5ccc66e081809346c319b9b31"}],"required_material_ids":["README.md:1:86","paxos/paxos.go:161:397","paxos/paxos.go:24:160","paxos/paxos.go:398:666","paxos/paxos.go:667:736","replica/replica.go:1:300","replica/replica.go:301:577","run.go:1:103"],"projection_level":0,"source_rule":"Orientation, provenance IDs and declaration hints are navigation, not source evidence. Only attached exact source supports current implementation judgments.","selected_question":{"disposition":"concrete_suspicion","preferred_check":"source_review","requests":[{"file":"replica/replica.go","start_line":1,"end_line":300,"reason":"Inspect shared replica construction, participant identity, quorum definitions, and peer connection ownership. Establish which configurations can require two or more remote acceptance contributions and whether transport preserves response identity."},{"file":"replica/replica.go","start_line":301,"end_line":577,"reason":"Locate and inspect SendMsg, peer decoding and RPC-channel delivery, and peer-order construction. Determine whether repeated application-level Accept broadcasts produce separately delivered replies from the same participant, and identify any filtering or serialization protection."},{"file":"run.go","start_line":1,"end_line":103,"reason":"Establish the selected Paxos deployment's participant-count and failure-parameter construction and which shared-runtime options are actually enabled."},{"file":"README.md","start_line":1,"end_line":86,"reason":"Look for attributed deployment, fault, and quorum expectations applicable to this Paxos branch. Documentation will support a contract only where its actual text establishes applicability."}],"question":"When handleAcceptReply consumes an equal-ballot AcceptReply, what establishes that the counted response represents acceptance of this instance's candidate commands by a distinct participant in the current attempt, rather than only a report of the recipient's current ballot?","importance":"Repeated replies from one participant could reach the local commitment threshold without the intended number of supporting participants. A ballot report retaining different commands could also be interpreted too strongly. Either would undermine the acceptance support used to authorize commitment; divergent decisions, durability failures, and client-visible consequences remain unestablished.","source_ids":["paxos/paxos.go:24:160","paxos/paxos.go:161:397","paxos/paxos.go:398:666","paxos/paxos.go:667:736"],"participants":["Replica issuing Prepare and Accept","Replica handling Accept","Replica handling PrepareReply and AcceptReply","Shared peer transport and RPC dispatcher"],"objects":["Accept and AcceptReply messages","Per-instance commands, bal, vbal, and status","Leader bookkeeping prepareOKs, acceptOKs, and lastTriedBallot","ReadQuorumSize and WriteQuorumSize","PreferredPeerOrder and Alive"],"contexts":["One instance and its current acceptance attempt","Additional equal-ballot PrepareReply deliveries after the prepare threshold","Multiple Accept broadcasts for the same instance and ballot","Accept handling with existing accepted or committed state","Attempt initialization, retry, and recovery","Transport delivery and actual quorum configuration remain unresolved"],"event_paths":["Prepare threshold reached -> Accept broadcast -> another equal-ballot PrepareReply -> another Accept broadcast -> one recipient handles both requests -> equal-ballot replies increment the same acceptOKs counter","Candidate command installation -> recipient guards and retained state -> AcceptReply construction -> ballot comparison -> local commitment threshold","Execution recovery request -> recover reuses existing bookkeeping -> makeBallot and Prepare broadcast -> reply processing"],"activity_classes":["A1"],"behavior_ids":["B_accept","B_accept_reply","B_prepare_reply","paxos_run_dispatch"],"fact_ids":["F_accept_reply_metadata"],"obligation_relation_kind":"consumption","counterevidence":["The run loop serially dispatches protocol handlers, so repeated broadcasts can be investigated without assuming concurrent execution of those handlers.","Each bcastAccept invocation visits at most N-1 peer-order entries and skips peers marked not alive. Distinct entries and transport behavior still require source inspection.","handleAccept installs incoming commands for missing or eligible noncommitted records; those replies follow actual command installation.","handleAccept rejects lower ballots and preserves committed records. An equal-ballot report with different retained commands still requires a justified reachable history.","handleAcceptReply ignores lower-ballot replies, treats higher ballots as nacks, and returns for committed instances. These checks restrict counting but do not visibly distinguish contributors before commitment.","handlePropose initializes fresh counters, and recover initializes counters when bookkeeping is absent. Existing bookkeeping is reused by recover.","A repeated response is consequential to distinct-participant support only when the applicable write threshold requires more distinct remote participants than have actually accepted. The deployed threshold remains to be verified.","Shared transport or dispatch filtering could constrain the suspected sequence and remains unread."],"unknowns":["Whether a legally reachable committed recipient can report the attempted ballot while retaining commands different from the current candidate.","Whether one instance and ballot always identifies one command batch across prepare completion, retry, recovery, and intervening handlers.","Whether the shared transport and RPC dispatcher deliver separately generated replies from repeated Accept broadcasts without contributor filtering.","Whether PreferredPeerOrder contains each remote participant exactly once.","Which deployment configurations require at least two remote contributions and permit the proposed message-processing order.","How attempt validity is maintained when existing counters survive recovery or ballot changes; visible recovery and retry paths do not reset them.","What establishes the candidate-command association of the implicit local contribution across all relevant paths.","The applicable acceptance-support contract and an independent oracle connecting the commitment threshold to distinct participant acceptance."],"priority":1,"trigger_rationale":"The newly attached source supplies an application-level source of repeated replies: handlePrepareReply invokes bcastAccept whenever prepareOKs+1 is at least ReadQuorumSize, without a one-shot phase guard. Additional equal-ballot prepare replies can therefore initiate additional Accept broadcasts. handleAccept replies on every invocation, and handleAcceptReply increments a scalar counter without visible contributor identity. This does not require assuming network duplication. A discriminating prospective schedule delivers multiple prepare replies, then processes replies to repeated Accept requests from one recipient before other recipients' acceptance. Whether that schedule can reach an insufficiently supported commitment depends on the actual quorum configuration and transport path, which require the requested source."},"source_receipt":{"id":"d872abeabf7745ea8888184513ac3f7d","snapshot_id":"5180759aeb0b40738fc40c8ed67623ed","purpose":"depth","status":"complete","items":[{"request":{"file":"replica/replica.go","start_line":1,"end_line":300,"reason":"Inspect shared replica construction, participant identity, quorum definitions, and peer connection ownership. Establish which configurations can require two or more remote acceptance contributions and whether transport preserves response identity."},"status":"cached","material_ids":["replica/replica.go:1:300"],"new_chars":0,"new_chunks":0,"reason":"","file_metadata":{"file":"replica/replica.go","lines":577,"content_digest":"ea28e2b94198a705e6dd9892a6154ce9b8cb424ec3dedeed8f8bd2488341e766","snapshot_id":"5180759aeb0b40738fc40c8ed67623ed","encoding":"utf-8"}},{"request":{"file":"replica/replica.go","start_line":301,"end_line":577,"reason":"Locate and inspect SendMsg, peer decoding and RPC-channel delivery, and peer-order construction. Determine whether repeated application-level Accept broadcasts produce separately delivered replies from the same participant, and identify any filtering or serialization protection."},"status":"cached","material_ids":["replica/replica.go:301:577"],"new_chars":0,"new_chunks":0,"reason":"","file_metadata":{"file":"replica/replica.go","lines":577,"content_d